In article <19JUL199211210917@skyblu.ccit.arizona.edu> lippard@skyblu.ccit.arizona.edu (James J. Lippard) writes:
>
>I've learned a little bit more about the case.  Amphitheatre High School
>in Tucson prohibits T-shirts and buttons taking any stance on the
>abortion issue.  The principal claims that they would be disruptive.

In the absence of strong evidence (as in, from experience) that the mere
wearing of such T-shirts or buttons actually *would* cause substantial
disruption, this is clearly a violation of the precedent set by Tinker.

From the majority opinion (written by Abe Fortas):

  "In order for the State in the person of school officials to justify
prohibition of a particular expression of opinion, it must be able to show
that its action was caused by something more than a mere desire to avoid the
discomfort and unpleasantness that always accompany an unpopular viewpoint.
Certainly where there is no finding and no showing that engaging in the
forbidden conduct would 'materially and substantially interfere with the
requirements of appropriate discipline in the operation of the school,' the
prohibition cannot be sustained..."


